{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4d1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e59-f410-76d3-be05-ab6d05f2f618"},"sequence":195,"recorded_at":1780531400000385,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f"},"call_id":"call_01a06e5a73787d62ae6bb531747f5715","text":"Read text file `.spec/README.md`.\n1|# AX v0.5.0 Implementation Source and Board Map\n2|\n3|The implementation board is derived from the published normative AX\n4|specification, not from mutable prose in this repository.\n5|\n6|## Pinned authority\n7|\n8|- Repository: `relux-works/agent-session-manager-spec`\n9|- Release: `v0.5.0`\n10|- Annotated tag object: `d3da6614a6c7bf119a88c9596a86c0853c22cfb9`\n11|- Peeled commit: `28bf96d7dd7ebf3cd9e2ccd91d35b8660699dd5c`\n12|- `SPEC.md` SHA-256: `562546d240f0fa3e71b47e6359a002f9892c0efd97e19eb55917527552ac484a`\n13|- Normative source: <https://github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager-spec/blob/v0.5.0/SPEC.md>\n14|\n15|The upstream specification remains normative. This file records the consumed\n16|release and implementation ownership; it does not amend or supersede the\n17|specification. The repository vendors one byte-exact, digest-verified copy of\n18|`SPEC.md` under `internal/specdoc` purely so repository gates can compare their\n19|artifacts against the real text; that copy carries no authority of its own.\n20|\n21|The implementation consumes this identity through the embedded\n22|[`internal/specpin/v0.5.0.lock.json`](../internal/specpin/v0.5.0.lock.json)\n23|lock. The lock records the ordered 60-row Section 1.5 registry, the exact\n24|v0.4.3 compatibility delta, and these shipped fixture identities:\n25|\n26|| Fixture ID | Upstream path | SHA-256 |\n27|| --- | --- | --- |\n28|| `ax-session-directory-conformance-v1` | `fixtures/session_directory_conformance.json` | `a6351a83e25a3a909297ed20bd1f4a75622b10f536a06b164fff3b12cb66f2ce` |\n29|| `ax-terminal-backend-conformance-v1` | `fixtures/terminal_backend_conformance.json` | `67de0d78d76c9c445c742af5c4c14ffa5cecd620d4cb07dc5497d391b421ad37` |\n30|| `ax-v0.4.3-roadmap-terminal-realm-v1` | `fixtures/v0_4_3_roadmap_terminal_realm.json` | `6023ec0d1562e8868b8bef3dc41cfd66ea0b4a4054fbaf13d3aec504578a7f74` |\n31|\n32|`internal/specpin.Current` and `internal/specpin.Verify` reject partial reads,\n33|unknown fields, substituted source identities, contract drift, fixture drift,\n34|and any byte-different lock.\n35|\n36|[`internal/specdoc`](../internal/specdoc) additionally vendors the byte-exact\n37|`SPEC.md` of this release as a verification input for repository fidelity gates,\n38|accepted only when its SHA-256 equals the digest above. The upstream\n39|specification remains normative: that copy is compared against, never amended,\n40|extended, or republished as authority, and only test binaries read it.\n41|\n42|This slice is read-only: it mutates no durable state and advertises no\n43|provider, platform, backend, or CLI capability.\n44|\n45|## Program shape\n46|\n47|The board contains five agent-executable implementation Epics and one separate\n48|advisory human-validation Epic:\n49|\n50|| Milestone | Board Epic | Stories | Tasks | Fibonacci points |\n51|| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n52|| M0 — contract foundation | `EPIC-260830-37rgqn` | 10 | 30 | 211 |\n53|| M1 — single-host durability | `EPIC-260830-1uwj54` | 12 | 36 | 342 |\n54|| M2 — multi-host preview | `EPIC-260830-3v9jlg` | 12 | 36 | 370 |\n55|| M3 — daily-driver tmux | `EPIC-260830-27bix9` | 7 | 21 | 205 |\n56|| M4 — cloning, Directory, platforms, release | `EPIC-260830-391tge` | 21 | 63 | 744 |\n57|| Advisory human validation | `EPIC-260830-1ewjla` | 4 | 12 | 49 |\n58|\n59|The automated implementation scope is 62 Stories, 186 Tasks, and 1,872\n60|Fibonacci points. Every implementation Story contains three atomic leaf Tasks\n61|with explicit scope, acceptance criteria, three checklist gates, an estimate,\n62|and a required review policy.\n63|\n64|These implementation counts intentionally exclude the M0 planning-only\n65|quality-assurance Story and its two independent reviewer Tasks. Those Tasks\n66|validate this decomposition; they do not enter the product release closure or\n67|inflate implementation estimates.\n68|\n69|## Machine-derived critical path\n70|\n71|The canonical project plan derives this milestone path from real `blocked_by`\n72|edges:\n73|\n74|```text\n75|EPIC-260830-37rgqn (M0)\n76|  -> EPIC-260830-1uwj54 (M1)\n77|  -> EPIC-260830-3v9jlg (M2)\n78|  -> EPIC-260830-27bix9 (M3)\n79|  -> EPIC-260830-391tge (M4)\n80|```\n81|\n82|The dependency closure of final release Task `TASK-260830-55kcni` contains all\n83|186 implementation Tasks. Its machine-derived leaf critical path contains 66\n84|Tasks and terminates at signed release evidence/publication. Use:\n85|\n86|```bash\n87|.agents/bin/task-board q 'plan()'\n88|.agents/bin/task-board q 'plan(TASK-260830-55kcni, mode=related)'\n89|```\n90|\n91|The human-validation Epic has no incoming or outgoing hard dependency to any\n92|M0-M4 Task. It may run in parallel or later. Human studies may create newly\n93|triaged Bugs or product decisions, but cannot silently block, complete, or\n94|rewrite automated implementation/conformance work.\n95|\n96|## Normative coverage map\n97|\n98|| SPEC.md scope | Primary implementation ownership |\n99|| --- | --- |\n100|| §1 conformance, registry, common rules | M0 source/catalog, common types, records, CI |\n101|| §2 product/operator model | M0 records; M1 state/name/profile; M3 routing |\n102|| §3 architecture/local layout/SQLite | M0 storage; M1 services |\n103|| §4 TerminalBackend/tmux/ConPTY/services | M0 TerminalBackend; M1 pane/tmux/Aqua/services; M4 Windows |\n104|| §5 domain records/state/ownership | M0 records; M1 state/lease/checkpoint/provider identity |\n105|| §6 Configuration 1/2/3 | M0 configuration |\n106|| §7 Provider, Session Adapter, Directory Node protocols | M0 provider/terminal/companion frameworks; provider stories in M4 |\n107|| §8 provider/platform contracts | M1 Codex/Claude gates; M3 lanes; M4 Windows/Gemini/Muse/Antigravity/Pi/Qwen |\n108|| §9 task-board integration | M1 task-board bridge; M4 provider/task-board acceptance |\n109|| §10 immutable objects/workspace/cloning/directory | M0 storage; M1 workspace; M2 ingestion/conflicts; M4 cloning/Directory |\n110|| §11 mesh RPC/replication | M2 peer/RPC/Merkle/transfer/ingestion; M4 directory mesh |\n111|| §12 workspace replication | M1 Git capture/materialization/groups; M2 transfer |\n112|| §13 lifecycle/recovery/cloning/continuation | M1 local lifecycle; M2 transfer/takeover/recovery; M3 restore; M4 cloning/planner/executor |\n113|| §14 CLI/operator experience | M0 CLI envelopes; M1/M2/M3 CLI; M4 query/TUI |\n114|| §15 errors/exits | M0 errors; every mutation/transport story tests mappings |\n115|| §16 security/threat boundary | M0 security primitives; M1 credential realm; M2 mesh; M3/M4 security gates |\n116|| §17 compatibility/migration | M0 config/errors/catalog; M2 RPC; M4 fleet compatibility |\n117|| §18 observability/operations | M1 events; M2 mesh health; M3 recovery views; M4 operations/retention |\n118|| §19 implementation conformance/release | M0-M4 exit/gate Stories and final release closure |\n119|| §20 specification publication/governance | M0 pinned source/traceability; M4 release evidence/governance |\n120|| Appendices A-D | M0 source/CI traceability; all provider/platform fixtures; M4 final conformance |\n121|\n122|## Execution rules\n123|\n124|- Agents execute the live task-board DAG; Markdown snapshots are recovery and\n125|  review artifacts only.\n126|- Implementation starts from unblocked `to-dev` Tasks and follows dependency\n127|  waves. It does not wait for the advisory human Epic.\n128|- Every production change uses the Curator-managed `go-testing-tools` skill,\n129|  task-scoped Story worktrees, a producer/reviewer cycle, and a pull request.\n130|- Closed historical wire contracts are preserved. Unknown or unproven\n131|  platform/provider/backend capabilities remain disabled and visible.\n132|- No implementation claim exists merely because a board Task is present.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
