{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000cfd1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e59-f410-76d3-be05-ab6d05f2f618"},"sequence":1603,"recorded_at":1780531400003201,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"e5a15eda-1664-4cc2-a36a-874b8661fa86"},"task_id":"e5a15eda-1664-4cc2-a36a-874b8661fa86","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"e5a15eda-1664-4cc2-a36a-874b8661fa86","chunk":"Read text file `README.md`.\n(359 line(s) above)\n360|backend implementation version registers, which refuses any settings object it\n361|did not declare. Section 6.4's \"no v2 table accepts a secret, endpoint\n362|credential, model token, auth root, or arbitrary environment passthrough\" is\n363|likewise a field-declaration rule, enforced by the closed table shape\n364|`decodeStrict` decodes with `DisallowUnknownFields`; Section 6.1 states the\n365|value rule and its permission together, forbidding secret *values* in config\n366|fields while allowing a provider to name a machine-local environment variable or\n367|credential profile.\n368|\n369|Run the focused tests and assigned-scope traceability gate with:\n370|\n371|    go test ./internal/config -count=1 -v\n372|    go test ./internal/config -cover -count=1\n373|    go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck -section 6.2\n374|\n375|Section 6.2 is the only configuration section the assigned-scope gate admits:\n376|its single normative clause is enumerated and discharged. Sections 3.2, 6.1,\n377|6.3, 6.4, 6.5, 17.1, 17.2 and 17.4 are refused with their measured ratio, and\n378|Section 6.5 additionally names the `required_capabilities` default defect. See\n379|[Specification-to-Code Ownership Gate](#specification-to-code-ownership-gate).\n380|\n381|## Canonical JSON and Immutable Object Identities\n382|\n383|[`internal/canonicaljson`](internal/canonicaljson) exposes the production RFC\n384|8785 JCS entry point and the AX immutable-object identity calculation. The JCS\n385|path validates I-JSON input before canonicalization, including duplicate-name,\n386|UTF-8, surrogate-pair, ECMAScript number-formatting, string-escaping, and\n387|UTF-16 property-ordering rules. Its number behavior is checked against every\n388|finite RFC 8785 Appendix B sample through the production entry point.\n389|\n(more lines below)"}}}
