{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000d061","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06e59-f410-76d3-be05-ab6d05f2f618"},"sequence":1675,"recorded_at":1780531400003345,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1619d586-4e11-48b5-9a4b-4a908ec4863f"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"6332003d-4045-4fce-9812-026add0fbaee"},"task_id":"6332003d-4045-4fce-9812-026add0fbaee","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"6332003d-4045-4fce-9812-026add0fbaee","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-53-1\",\n  \"command\": \"head -40 LOGBOOK.md; echo ...; tail -20 LOGBOOK.md\",\n  \"description\": \"Check logbook format\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"# Flight Logbook\\n\\n> Institutional memory. Concise, factual, high-signal.\\n> Newest entries first. One block per insight.\\n\\n## 2026-09-03\\n\\n### 1632 — The generalisation was written down one round earlier and still not executed: a list is a forced traversal, prose is a memory\\n\\n- SCOPE: new `internal/cliresult/guards_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/guard_inventory_test.go`, one production fix in `internal/cliresult/client.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-7 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §1.6, §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §17.2.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: the `ErrUnregisteredExitStatus` gate in `cliresult.Read` was driven at four sampled values — `{1, 42, -1, 255}` — while its twin `axerror.decodeExitStatus`, which has the same shape and the same domain, was swept over 0..255 in the round-6 rework one file away. Restricting the reader gate to exactly its four covered values left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages, as did sparing 127 and 137 — the two statuses a wrapper most plausibly returns when `ax` never ran. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head with the delete-only control KILLED in the same run.\\n- FINDING: entry 1447 below states the exact generalisation this round violated — \\\"when a class of 'proven at one sample' is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named\\\". It was written and then not executed. The orchestrator asked for the enumerated list, accepted a sweep without it on the reasoning that the table was a means rather than an end, and the omission it would have made visible cost a whole review round. A generalisation recorded as prose is a memory; a list is a traversal. Working from memory closes the guard you were told about.\\n- FIX: `TestTheRefusalGuardInventoryIsDerivedFromTheReaderSource` parses every refusal site out of `internal/cliresult/client.go` with `go/ast` and requires a bijection with a published README table — twelve rows, each naming its function, sentinel, refusal marker, domain-evidence class from a closed vocabulary, and the test declarations that measure it. A guard added to the reader with no row is a red; a row whose marker no longer resolves is a red. A second table enumerates the guards this leaf added or reordered outside that file, `decodeExitStatus` required in it by name, because the twin one file away is exactly what a single-file table would have missed again.\\n- FINDING: the traversal immediately produced three more instances of the class, none of which any reviewer had named. The `ErrForeignDocument` branch was covered by one schema identifier, so restricting it to that identifier and routing every other foreign schema into `readSuccess` survived the suite. The \\\"schema member is not a string\\\" guard was covered by the number `5`, so restricting it to JSON numbers survived. Both are the round-6 shape: not an admission bypass, a change in WHICH FACT the machine client is told. The presence guard for a missing `schema` member survives an arbitrary predicate keyed on the exit status, which is the class round 4 declined to block on and is recorded as a stated bound rather than fixed.\\n- FIX (production, the only behaviour change this round): `encoding/json` admits JSON `null` into a `string` and yields `\\\"\\\"`, so a document whose `schema` member was `null` had been answered as a FOREIGN CONTRACT carrying the schema `\\\"\\\"` — a claim that some other contract owns the document, when what is true is that this one is not readable. Found only because the type guard's domain was swept rather than sampled. The guard now checks the raw token alongside the unmarshal.\\n- DECISION: the round-6 reviewer asked whether `exitStatusIsNotEnough` should distinguish \\\"this status carries no registered code\\\" from \\\"this status is not in the table\\\", since it computes `len(fanIn[status])` with no membership check and would report a map miss as a measured 0. The answer is not a defensive branch: a branch no production path can take promises nothing and is never measured. `TestTheFanInSentenceCannotReportAMapMissAsAMeasurement` proves the fabrication unreachable instead — `documentSchema` has exactly one caller and it is `Read`, the gate precedes that call in `Read`'s body by AST position, and every (registered version, registered failure status) pair measures at least one code, so a 0 there could only ever have been a miss. Moving the gate behind the discriminator reddens it.\\n- FINDING (reviewer observation, disclosed rather than fixed): the §1.6 common-data-model gate does NOT enforce the number half of that section. `Read` admits a Structured Error whose `details` carry `1.5`, which SPEC.md forbids as integers inside the IEEE 754 safe-integer range. The gate's doc comment enumerates what it owns honestly; its opening sentence and README's matching phrasing overstated it. §1.6's number rule is bound to `internal/scalar` and left unevidenced there, so nothing false is claimed in the traceability projection. The admission is now ASSERTED by a test, so closing the gap reddens the pin and forces the disclosure to be updated in the same change — a disclosure with no pin decays into a paragraph nobody re-reads.\\n- FINDING (reviewer observation, correction to the record): three mutants were dismissed in round 5 on the grounds that \\\"none is a registered code\\\". `terminal_backend_capability_unproven` IS registered at Structured Error 1.3.0 exit 6 (`catalog_gen.go:541`), and sparing it in `ExitCodeFor`'s version scoping survives on a clean tree. The conclusion holds for a different reason — the same narrowing on `observation_gap` is killed by `TestExitCodeForRefusesVersionAndCodeDrift`, so the class has a covered representative. The dismissal stands; the sentence behind it did not. A survivor dismissed with a wrong reason is worse than one dismissed with none, because the reason is what the next round trusts.\\n- DECISION: reviewer observation O3 was closed by measurement rather than by the stated bound the reviewer offered. `verifyEnvelopeIdentity`'s major comparison was proven at major 2 alone; it is now swept over majors 0..64 through `Read` with the bound stated for the tail. Where a domain can be swept cheaply, a stated bound is a bound about effort, and this leaf has published the rule that a bound must be about the contract.\\n- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All eight new tests register against the existing `cli-result-machine-reading` acceptance case rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; the registration was probed by renaming a declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 63 to 86 mutants — 85 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 64 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green after every batch. Supersedes the \\\"63 mutants at 62 killed\\\" figure in the 1447 entry below. One mutant on the §1.6 disclosure was DISCARDED as equivalent and replaced: disabling the assertion in the test changes no outcome while the gap is open, so the replacement closes the gap in production and requires the pin to redden. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.\\n\\n### 1447 — The exit status a document declares is a member, not a fact: a guard proven on one code let a relabelled envelope forge a retry claim\\n\\n- SCOPE: new `internal/axerror/agreement_test.go` and `internal/cliresult/relabel_test.go`, `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-6 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.1, §15.2, §15.3. No production code changed — every round-6 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.\\n- ROOT CAUSE: the code-to-exit-status agreement in `axerror.decodeBody`, `if exitCode != expectedExit`, was covered by exactly one row of `TestDecodeRefusesClosedShapeViolations`: `observation_gap` driven at exit 5 instead of its own exit 9. One code, one wrong status, out of 752 ordered (code, wrong-status) pairs at 1.0.0 and 1,744 at 1.3.0. Narrowing the guard to `code == \\\"observation_gap\\\"` — restricting it to exactly the pair that row drives — left `go test ./... -count=1` at exit 0 across all 13 packages; so did sparing `policy_refused` or `authentication_failed` individually. Only deleting the guard reddened anything. Reproduced SURVIVED at the reviewed head before a line of test code was written.\\n- FINDING: this was a reachable bypass and not only an unmeasured ratio. `code` and `exit_code` are two independent members of one document, and this guard is the sole thing binding them. The retryability refusal this package publishes keys on the EXIT STATUS for three whole classes — 7 authorization, 16 policy refusal, 130 interrupt — so relabelling a code out of its own class asks that refusal about the wrong class and it stays silent. Under the narrowing, `{\\\"code\\\":\\\"authentication_failed\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":9,\\\"retryable\\\":true}` was ADMITTED by `cliresult.Read` at exit status 9, and the caller received a `Reading` naming an authorization failure with `Retryable() == true`. The §14.2 exit-status corroboration cannot see it, because the process really did exit with the relabelled status.\\n- FINDING: this is the FOURTH guard on this leaf published proven at one sample, after both `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards in entry 1231 and the command-tag agreement in entry 1421. Each round closed the guards it was shown and did not sweep the sibling on the same reading path. The generalisation, stated once: when a class of \\\"proven at one sample\\\" is found, the remedy is to enumerate every guard on that path and measure each over its own domain, not to fix the one that was named.\\n- FIX: `TestTheCodeToExitStatusAgreementIsMeasuredOverTheWholeCodeRegistry` drives all 316 registered codes across the 4 registered versions at all 17 registered failure statuses — 5,372 rows. The agreeing status must be ADMITTED and each of the other sixteen refused with this guard's own sentence naming both numbers, so a pair settled by `decodeExitStatus`, by the closed member set, or by the retryability gate does not count as coverage of it. Per-version totals are pinned at 752, 1,056, 1,504 and 1,744 and re-derived from `CodesFor` and `CodesByExitStatus`.\\n- DECISION: close the bypass at the production entry point as well as at the decoder, because they are different claims. `TestRelabellingACodeOutOfItsExitClassCannotForgeARetryClaimThroughRead` takes the 7 codes of Structured Error 1.0.0 whose own class carries an exit-keyed retryability refusal, relabels each into all 14 statuses that carry none, and requires `Read` to refuse all 98. The control in the other direction is required in the same loop — the same code at its own status with `retryable: true` must be refused by the retryability gate — which is what makes the relabelling a bypass of something rather than a rewrite of nothing.\\n- FINDING (reviewer observation, closed in the same pass): the registered-status admission in `decodeExitStatus` was driven at `{0, 1, 18, 99}`, so `!IsFailureExitStatus(status) && status != 42` survived the whole suite. The complement of a four-value sample is not a bound. `TestTheExitStatusAdmissionIsSweptOverEveryByteValue` sweeps 0..255 plus six out-of-range values and requires admission if and only if the §15.2 table registers the status as a failure.\\n- DECISION: an oracle must not be the predicate under test. The sweep above enumerates the expected admissions through `ExitStatusMeaning`, not `IsFailureExitStatus`, because the latter IS the decision being measured and an oracle built from it would agree with whatever the mutant narrowed it to. The asserted row count of seventeen is what catches a mutation that moves both. The sweep also drives a code the registry does not carry, which takes the §15.3 unknown-code branch and skips the agreement guard, so exactly one decision is left in the row.\\n- ANOMALY: the two guards closed here are on the same function as gates this leaf has swept three times, and neither appeared in any of the 14-, 23-, 28-, 44- or 53-mutant harnesses. The harness grew by whatever the last review named. A hand-picked mutant set measures the reviewer's attention, not the code — the same shape entry 2113 records for a sweep that selects its own mutants.\\n- BOUND, unchanged and restated: `tracecheck` still reports `acceptance_cases=74` and `clauses_discharged=17/403`. All three new tests register against existing acceptance cases rather than new ones, so no coverage figure moved; each registration was probed by renaming its declaration and confirming `tracecheck` names the missing declaration ahead of the projection digest.\\n- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 53 to 63 mutants — 62 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 44 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green. Supersedes the \\\"53 mutants at 52 killed\\\" figure in the 1421 entry below. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. TASK-260830-33sfxc revision 6, handed to review.\\n\\n### 1421 — A guard proven at one ordered pair admits a forged authority document, and the same blind spot sits in the sibling reader\\n\\n- SCOPE: `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (one subtest rewritten into a both-directions sweep, one new grid test), `internal/cliresult/datamodel_test.go` and `internal/axerror/datamodel_test.go` (large-document rows), `README.md`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-5 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §1.6. No production code changed — every round-5 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.\\n...\\n### 2129 — Derived-completeness gate blind to its own refusal class\\n- ROOT CAUSE: `internal/localstore/projection_refusal_inventory_test.go` derived expected refusal sites from the production AST but matched only `*ast.Ident` calls named `projectionRefusal`. Six live clauses written as bare `fmt.Errorf(\\\"%w: ...\\\", ErrUnsafeOwnership)` or as delegated `verifyOwnerFileInfo`/`verifyOwnerDirectory` guards were invisible to the gate whose job was to notice them, and shipped unpinned while 20+ funnelled sites were enforced.\\n- FINDING: A completeness gate over a *pattern* must also police the pattern. Deriving the expected set is not enough if the extraction rule silently excludes a whole syntactic class.\\n- FIX: Second funnel `projectionOwnershipRefusal` (`internal/localstore/projection.go:117`) keeps the refusal decision at its call site while preserving the owner-only cause; every projection refusal rerouted through a funnel. Gate now derives two properties — funnel sites need an exercised negative path beneath `openProjection`, AND no projection-owned source may raise a refusal outside a funnel. Build-context filtering added so a platform-specific refusal is not demanded from a platform that cannot run it.\\n- ANOMALY: I reproduced the identical bug class inside the fix. The new unrouted check sat behind `typed.Fun.(*ast.Ident)`, and `fmt.Errorf` is a `*ast.SelectorExpr`, so the exact class stayed invisible in code written specifically to catch it. First run of mutant m41 exited 0. Reading the new gate would not have found this; only the behaviour-preserving mutant did.\\n- DECISION: Assume \\\"unreachable\\\" last, not first. One clause presumed unreachable — the non-regular branch of `ensureProjectionFileModes` — is drivable through the real production entry by replacing a sidecar with a FIFO in the post-rebuild hook. Only three clauses are genuine TOCTOU re-checks, each annotated with the earlier check that owns the reachable case.\\n- SCOPE: `internal/localstore/projection.go`, `projection_lock_unix.go`, `projection_refusal_inventory_test.go`, new `projection_unix_test.go`, `README.md`.\\n- STATUS: Resolved. 15 mutants, 13 red as required, 1 designed control green, 1 documented subsumed green. TASK-260830-3amrl9 rev5, handed to review.\\n\\n### 2113 — A gate must prove its own coverage; so must a mutation sweep\\n- ROOT CAUSE: `requireMemberSet` was a byte-identical duplicate of `requireExactMembers`. `TestConstraintEnumerationMatchesRequireExactMembers` scanned only the latter by name, so `WorkspaceMember.*`, every Session Event payload, `Observation Event`, and `ObservationCounts` carried zero per-member enumeration rows while the README claimed a complete inventory.\\n- FINDING: That blind spot is why a real defect reached review — `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go` `validateTerminalV4Payload` validated `terminal_backend_id` with `requireString` only, so `CalculateObjectIdentity` attested `AX.TMUX`, `../../etc/passwd`, an embedded NUL and 5000 chars. SPEC.md:890 (§4.B) pins it to 1–128 ASCII bytes matching `[a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:[.-][a-z0-9]+)*`.\\n- FIX: Duplicate deleted, all 5 call sites collapsed onto `requireExactMembers`. Grammar enforced by `requireTerminalBackendID`. `TestRequireExactMembersIsTheOnlyClosedMemberGate` now derives every function emitting a closed-member refusal and fails unless that set is exactly `{requireExactMembers}`; the scanned file set is derived from the package directory, not a hand-written list.\\n- ANOMALY: The mutation harness aborted mid-run on the zsh reserved variable `status`, after applying mutant 1 but before its restore. The next run took its baseline backup from that mutated tree, so all 12 mutants were behavioural no-ops against an already-red mask and every one reported KILLED for the wrong reason. Caught only because the full-repo run afterwards failed.\\n- FIX: Harness refuses to sweep unless the baseline is green, and reports `NOT-APPLIED` for any mutant that left the tree unchanged. Re-run: 12/12 killed, 0 survivors.\\n- DECISION: Session Event payload members are pinned by mechanical extraction from the pinned SPEC's four `Exact payload members` tables into `internal/canonicaljson/testdata/session-event-payload-members.md` (39 event types, 204 declarations) rather than hand-listed across 134 version×event shapes.\\n- FINDING: The first extractor split on `</code>,` and silently dropped members followed by a bare `(G2)` annotation, losing `clone.planned.projection_plan_id` and `clone.failed.materialization_id`. The new derivation test caught it; review had not. Parse one `<code>` span per declaration.\\n- NOTE: General property, applies to every derived gate — an inventory must assert that the set of constructs it scans is complete for the package, and a mutation sweep must assert its baseline is green. A gate that cannot fail when someone adds a second copy of a check is decoration, and a mutant that \\\"kills\\\" an already-red mask proves nothing.\\n- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/`, `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `README.md`\\n- STATUS: Resolved; TASK-260830-1tax26 handed to review as revision 2.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 19178,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 4795,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}"}}}
